\begin{abstract}
This interim report summarizes progress toward solving SemEval-2026 Task 12 on Abductive Reasoning. We describe the problem, datasets, baseline models, prompting strategies, and preliminary results. The goal is to outline current achievements and identify next steps.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Abductive reasoning requires models to infer the most plausible explanation for a given incomplete context. SemEval-2026 Task 12 focuses on multiple-choice abductive reasoning across narrative scenarios and noisy distractor documents. This report presents our methods, baseline experimentation, and preliminary findings.

\section{Task Description}
The task provides:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Context (observations, questions, and distractor documents)
    \item Multiple-choice questions to select the most plausible explanation
    \item Evaluation using accuracy
\end{itemize}

\section{Dataset}
We use:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{val\_data}: official validation dataset
    \item \textbf{sample\_data}: smaller subset for quick benchmarking
\end{itemize}

Each instance includes context, question, and four candidate answers.

\section{Baseline Models}
\subsection{Qwen}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Model: Qwen3-30B-A3B-Thinking-2507-GGUF
    \item Strong reasoning, zero-shot English performs best
\end{itemize}

\subsection{LLaMA}
\begin{itemize}
    \item LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct
    \item Lightweight, instruction-following, suitable for multiple-choice
\end{itemize}

\subsection{DeBERTa}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Encoder model; no multiple-choice head
    \item Encode (question + option) separately and pick the highest score
\end{itemize}

\section{Prompting Strategies}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Zero-shot prompts
    \item Few-shot prompts
    \item Expert-style prompts: Causal, Evidence-driven, Domain-specific
    \item Language variation: English vs. Chinese
\end{itemize}

